                <pb facs="0821" n="507" sameAs="frus:frus1914Supp/pg507"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/2</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                    <placeName>
                        <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1914-09-02">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">September 2, 1914</hi>.</date>
                    <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8:30 p.m.</hi>] </dateline>
                <p rendition="#center">[Telegram]</p>
                <p>558. Your telegram of August 23, 8 p.m. The Foreign Office now informs me that
                    there is nothing in the censorship regulations to prevent the correction of
                    errors made in transmission in the usual manner provided for in the
                    international telegraph regulations. If the matter is to be taken up further the
                    telegraph company must give particular instances in which censors are alleged to
                    have refused to pass service messages for such correction.</p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
